<h2 id="values">Keep each value type</h2>

Number keeps its minimum, maximum and step, and its slider or box mode. Boolean stays a true or false value, not the words “yes” and “no”. Date, datetime, time, duration and period are stored differently, and a duration is not a clock time. RGB colour is channel values. Colour temperature has its own units and device limits. Do not convert one into the other to reuse a control.

Select keeps single or multiple choice, its labelled options and its rule on custom values. Choose shows the selected branch and its dependent fields. Keep a stored inactive branch only if the native schema does. A placeholder is not a label and not a default answer. Entity name and state content selectors set what is displayed. They do not change which entity is meant.

Device class, state class and unit of measurement limit how data is read. Explain how they relate and keep their filters. Changing a displayed unit does not convert recorded values. See [Charts and data](../content/formatting-content/charts-and-data.html) and [Locale, units and time](locale-units-and-time.html).

<%= example('ha-coverage-selector-values') %>

<h2 id="structured">Actions, conditions, triggers and structured input</h2>

Action, condition and trigger selectors use typed editors, not plain text. A trigger starts a check, conditions decide whether actions run, and actions ask for work. The selector for selectors chooses a schema, not the value that follows. Automation behaviour and numeric threshold selectors are internal typed settings with their own limits. Keep advanced options easy to find without forcing anyone into code.

Object and template editors need a visible label, the expected shape, syntax feedback and the person's input kept after a rejection. A valid template can still give a value the receiver cannot use. Keep syntax checking, schema checking and running apart, and never run a draft just to check it. A file input says which content is allowed and where the upload goes before the upload starts. A QR code is an output or a specific selector, so offer a readable alternative and do not put secrets in it.

<%= example('ha-coverage-selector-structured') %>

<h2 id="presentation">Internal selectors and display settings</h2>

The pinned Home Assistant frontend handles 61 selector keys. The public blueprint documentation lists 41 of them. The [interface coverage register](interface-coverage.html) marks source-only selectors, app compatibility and the legacy `ui-action` and `ui-color` aliases. An internal key is not a recommendation to depend on an undocumented interface.

Icon, theme, UI colour, navigation, UI action, clock and date format, time format and areas display settings change presentation. Preview them without running the configured action. A theme preview must not change equipment. A navigation destination needs a valid context. A proposed action needs a named target and outcome. The examples here are plain HTML for one purpose each, not copies of the searchable native selectors.

<h2 id="errors">Validation and recovery</h2>

A missing value, an incompatible value, a missing resource and a backend failure need different messages. Tie each field error to its labelled control and link an error summary to the invalid input. Do not clear a password or a destructive change silently unless the security flow requires it and explains how to recover. An unset value, an explicit null and an empty string can mean different things, so keep the schema's meaning.

<h2 id="acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

Round-trip every supported selector value type through the native form. Test a missing default, removing from a multiple choice, a change of context, a saved resource that has disappeared and a backend rejection. Check that previewing or validating runs no code and no action. Test keyboard focus and error recovery in nested choose and object forms at 320px. Before using an internal key, review the pinned source and test native behaviour.

<h2 id="evidence">Where this page stops</h2>

This is a reference specification, not a replacement for native components. The examples use invented records and give local feedback only. They do not call Home Assistant. Mapping a selector to this page names its design owner. It does not show that every integration, browser or device has been tested. Keep native state formatting, translations, permissions and event handling. Theme tokens change presentation only.

The [interface coverage register](interface-coverage.html) records the baseline and the mappings. Record real tests with [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html). For layout, colour and type, see the [design styles](../styles/index.html). Keep 16px control text, visible labels, keyboard access and readable names at 320px, and do not remove an explanation or a recovery route to make a layout fit.

Baseline: Home Assistant Core 2026.11.1 and frontend 20261028.5. References, which open outside this site: [Selectors](https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/blueprint/selectors/), the [pinned selector dispatcher](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/src/components/ha-selector/ha-selector.ts) and the [pinned selector types](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/src/data/selector.ts). For the editors, see [Automation and script editing](automation-editing.html#ha-ctrl-009-capabilities).
